    "title": "NYT investigation names Adam Back as Bitcoin's Satoshi Nakamoto",
    "text": "John Carreyrou — the journalist who broke the Theranos story — spent 18 months trying to identify Satoshi Nakamoto, Bitcoin's pseudonymous creator. His conclusion, published in the New York Times this week: Adam Back, 55, a British cryptographer who invented Hashcash, the computational puzzle system that became Bitcoin's proof-of-work mining engine. Back has denied it. He produced emails from 2008 showing Satoshi contacted him as a stranger before publishing the Bitcoin white paper — apparently just asking to check a citation. Carreyrou suspects Back wrote those emails to himself as cover. Report AdWorking with Times AI editor Dylan Freedman, Carreyrou ran Satoshi's hyphenation errors — 325 instances of nonstandard usage — against 620 writers who had posted at least 10 times to cryptography mailing lists before Bitcoin's 2008 launch. Back matched 67 of those exact errors. The next closest writer matched 38. The circumstantial evidence: Back went nearly silent on the Cryptography mailing list during the years Satoshi was active, resurfaced six weeks after Satoshi vanished in 2011, and wrote posts a decade before Bitcoin outlining virtually every technical element of it — distributed nodes, Hashcash-based coin minting, inflation adjustment, immutable timestamping. Report AdAt a conference in El Salvador this year, when Carreyrou quoted a Satoshi line — \"I'm better with code than with words\" — Back responded: \"I did a lot of talking though for somebody, I mean…\" Carreyrou heard that as Back momentarily speaking as Satoshi. Back called it a conversational slip. Back has co-founded a Bitcoin treasury company that is currently in the process of going public through a merger with a shell created by Cantor Fitzgerald. As CEO, he would be required to disclose any material information to investors, which would include a billion-dollar bitcoin hoard, if it exists, according to the New York Times. Report AdPreviously: HBO Bitcoin documentary claims to unmask Sa